1. Overall design and theme
The website should look like a combination of a romantic digital scrapbook, a midnight birthday surprise, and a handwritten love letter.
- Theme: Dreamy, romantic, intimate, slightly magical.
- Colours: Midnight black, deep navy, muted purple, blush pink, warm cream, and soft gold.
- Typography: Elegant cursive headings paired with clean, readable body text.
- Decorations: Tiny stars, glowing hearts, subtle sparkles, Polaroid-style photos, handwritten notes, and soft animations.
- Mood: It should feel personal and mature rather than overly childish or covered in hearts.
- Layout: A long, scrollable page with smooth navigation, interactive sections, and hidden surprises.
The website should work well on both a phone and a laptop because he will probably open the link on his phone.
2. Detailed website structure









1. The welcome screen
First impression
This is the first thing he sees when he opens the link.
- A starry night background with subtle animated stars.
- A large heading: “Happy Birthday, My Love ♡”
- His name or nickname in elegant cursive.
- A short message explaining that you made something special for him.
- A button saying “Your little surprise awaits…”
- A subtle music toggle, if you want background music.
Interaction: Clicking the button smoothly takes him to the next section, perhaps with floating hearts or a little sparkle animation.












2. Our little universe
Memories
A gallery dedicated to your relationship.
- Your favourite photos together.
- Screenshots of funny conversations.
- Your favourite selfies of him.
- Captions explaining why each memory matters.
- Optional dates for important milestones.
Interaction: Clicking a photo opens it larger, with a handwritten caption underneath. A “See one more memory” button can reveal an additional photo or a secret note.

Use real memories rather than generic romantic quotes — that is what will make the website feel like it belongs to the two of you.












3. Things I adore about you
Personalised
Present a collection of reasons you love him as small cards, notes, or clickable hearts.

Examples:
- The way you make me laugh.
- How you listen when I need you.
- How you make me feel loved even from far away.
- Your little habits that I secretly adore.
- The way ordinary conversations with you become my favourite part of the day.
Interaction: Each heart reveals a new reason when clicked. You could create 10, 20, or even 50 reasons, with the last one being a particularly personal message.












4. Our story so far
Relationship timeline
A timeline of your relationship, from the beginning to the present.

Include milestones such as:
- The first time you talked.
- Your first memorable conversation.
- The first time you realised you liked him.
- Your funniest call or most memorable moment.
- An important anniversary or milestone.
- Something you are looking forward to doing together.
Interaction: Each timeline milestone can expand when clicked to reveal a photo, a memory, or a short paragraph.












5. His birthday countdown
Animated
A countdown displaying days, hours, minutes, and seconds until his birthday.

On his birthday, the countdown should change into a birthday greeting instead of continuing to count down.

Interaction: When the birthday arrives, the page can display a celebratory animation with confetti, glowing stars, and a message such as “IT'S YOUR DAY! 🎂”

If the website is opened after his birthday, it should still show a birthday greeting rather than a broken or confusing countdown.












6. The surprise room
Main attraction
This is the most interactive part of the website. Show him several mystery cards that he can open one by one.

Surprise A — A message from me: A hidden birthday message appears in a beautiful popup.

Surprise B — Our song: A button opens a playlist you created for him.

Surprise C — How well do you know us? A playful quiz about your relationship, with answers and personalised reactions.

Surprise D — Open when you miss me: A digital letter for moments when the distance feels difficult.

Surprise E — Your birthday wish: Ask him to make a wish before clicking a glowing star that reveals a final message.

Interaction: Each card opens a different surprise. You can also reveal the cards one at a time to make the experience feel like opening several gifts.












7. The final love letter
Emotional ending
This should be the most meaningful part of the website.

Use a cream-coloured paper design against the dark background, with a handwritten-style heading such as “Before you go, one last thing…”

Write a genuine letter telling him what he means to you, what you appreciate about him, and what you wish you could do for him on his birthday.

End with your signature, nickname, a heart, or a line that only the two of you understand.

Interaction: The letter can initially appear as a sealed envelope. When he clicks it, the envelope opens and the letter gradually appears.



3. Extra interactive features
These are optional features that can make the experience more special without making the website too complicated.
Background music

A music toggle that plays your chosen song after he clicks play. It should start only after a user interaction, since browsers often block automatic audio playback.



Floating hearts

Small hearts drift upward when he opens a surprise or clicks a special button.



A secret section

A surprise protected by a shared nickname, an anniversary date, or a little question only he knows the answer to. This is a playful reveal, not real security for private information.



A final gift reveal

At the end, a gift box opens to reveal a digital gift card, a special playlist, a video message, or a virtual date invitation.



A birthday celebration button

A button labelled “Make a wish” triggers confetti, glowing stars, and a final birthday message.



4. Suggested website flow
The experience should have a beginning, a build-up, and a meaningful ending.
1. Open the secret link

A personalised birthday welcome

2. Relive your memories

Photos, relationship timeline, reasons you love him

3. Explore the surprises

Quiz, playlist, hidden messages and birthday wishes

4. Open the final letter

Your personal message and a birthday wish from the heart


5. How to make it feel truly personal
The design is only half of the gift. The content should sound like you, not like a generic romantic website.
- Use the nickname you actually call him.
- Include three to six meaningful photos to start with.
- Add at least one inside joke.
- Include one memory that always makes you smile.
- Make the quiz questions specific to your relationship.
- Write the final letter in your natural voice.
- End with something unique to your relationship, such as a phrase you always say to each other.
Avoid adding too many sections just for the sake of features. A handful of thoughtful surprises will feel more meaningful than dozens of generic animations.
